{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Windows\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -LiteralPath 'archive/peer/2026-09-26-hyp-l2a1-p2-86-5.md' -Raw -Encoding UTF8; rg -n -A 20 -B 2 '\"'^194'\"\\\\.|P2 sr|P2 tun|sr1_L0|sr2_L0|sr3_L0|\\\"tun1\\\"|\\\"tun2\\\"' docs/d1-loop12-17-split-plan.md tools/bench/stage_d1_l2a1.json\"","aggregated_output":"# hyp-l2a1-p2-86-5\r\n\r\n- **agent:** claude\r\n- **role:** hypothesis\r\n- **model:** claude-opus-5-5 (effort high; peer.ps1 default for role hypothesis)\r\n- **kind:** review\r\n- **cost:** $1.1058  in 20 / out 8318 / cache-create 90442 / cache-read 965646  (97s, 15 turn(s))\r\n- **date:** 2026-09-26 00:07:05\r\n- **outcome:** ANSWERED (100s)\r\n- **verdict-card:** VERDICT-CARD hyp-l2a1-p2-86-5 verdict=refuted -> tools\\bench\\cards\\verdict_hyp-l2a1-p2-86-5.json\r\n- **why asked:** (Claude fills in)\r\n- **verdict:** unverified\r\n\r\n## Question\r\n\r\n--- REVIEW CARD (review/1, id hyp-l2a1-p2-86-5, role hypothesis) ---\r\nCLAIM: The 5 P2 Is Broken? FAILs in stage_d1_l2a1_86-5.log are an addressing defect in the recipe's P2 end (frame_diagram from resolve_addr on the checkpoint read L names Diagram[21], which does not hold the sinks), not a build defect.\r\nPREDICTED: P2 ordered second pass on sr1_L0/sr2_L0/sr3_L0/tun1/tun2: wire_delta 0 and Wire.Is Broken? False\r\nOBSERVED: stage_d1_l2a1_86-5.log:689-711: each 2nd pass raised 'RuntimeError: #<sink> not in Diagram[21].Nodes[]' in stagekit.address:740-741 before any connect; Is Broken? None; E1 42/42, PB cdiff == 9 open rows, RBW deletes no re-wired sink wire\r\nALREADY RULED OUT: plan/sim divergence at the checkpoints: E1 every real op == its step (log:660)\r\nALREADY RULED OUT: open-row mismatch: PB extra [] missing [] (log:~730)\r\nATTACHMENT: tools/bench/stage_d1_l2a1_86-5.log (md5 None)\r\nATTACHMENT: tools/recipes/stage_d1_l2a1.py (md5 None)\r\nATTACHMENT: tools/stagekit.py (md5 None)\r\nATTACHMENT: tools/bench/sim/l2a1/stageplan_l2a1.json (md5 None)\r\n--- END REVIEW CARD ---\r\n\r\nFAILED PREDICTION, card 86-5: tools/recipes/stage_d1_l2a1.py (md5 1098a443), log tools/bench/stage_d1_l2a1_86-5.log (one run, 594 s).\r\n\r\nTest: fresh LabVIEW, D1_k_20260925_100155.vi (md5 6cf5b077, unchanged after) -> D1_l2_a1_20260925_235224.vi, the\r\nfinalized plan_l2a1 (42 ops / 46 acts) through stagexec with PD193 checkpoints [15,19,23,27,28,40,41,42].\r\n\r\nPassed: E1 every real op's graph == its simulated step (42 ops) (log:660); READS only at the checkpoints, 0 stale\r\nretries (log:662); 4 CT ControlTerminal rows == simulated ends (log:665-671); FU frame-uid sets equal; PB frame-keyed\r\ncdiff == the plan's 9 open_rows, extra [] missing [] (log:~730); saved md5 51d9b8a3, 307992 B (log:748); RBW on a scratch\r\ndeletes 29 wires, none of a re-wired sink (log:757-758); refs 22/22; pins and input md5 unchanged. 27 pass / 5 fail.\r\n\r\nFailed: all 5 P2 `Wire.Is Broken?` gates (sr1_L0, sr2_L0, sr3_L0, tun1, tun2) - the ONLY P2 rows the recipe does not\r\nskip (kind tunnel/wire_sr with a non-ControlTerminal node sink, stage_d1_l2a1.py:82-91). Each second pass raised\r\nBEFORE any connect: \"RuntimeError: #5825 not in Diagram[21].Nodes[]\" (#5702, #10750, #5725, #5967 likewise, all\r\nDiagram[21]) (log:689-711), raised by Stage.address (tools/stagekit.py:740-741) called from cfw_second_pass\r\n(stagekit.py:848-864). So Is Broken? = None, and the \"wire_delta 0\" PASS beside each is vacuous (no connect ran).\r\nThis is the first run that ever reached P2 (no earlier stage_d1_l2a1*.log has a P2 sr/tun line).\r\n\r\nMy explanation: a READING/ADDRESSING defect in the recipe's P2 end, not a build defect. The recipe builds `end` with\r\n\"diagram\": r[\"frame_diagram\"] from SS.resolve_addr(L, a[\"dst\"], False) on the LAST checkpoint read L, and\r\nowner_class/term_class \"\" (stage_d1_l2a1.py:87-90). All five resolve to the same live Diagram[21], which does not hold\r\nthe sink nodes - so either resolve_addr's frame_diagram names the wrong diagram for these sinks (e.g. the loop/structure\r\ndiagram rather than the Case frame that owns them, or a pre-move location), or the uid->index match in address()\r\n(report_all('Diagram') by uid, stagekit.py:722-723) picks a diagram other than the one E1 used.\r\nCompeting explanation: the sinks really are not where the plan says - the build left these five sink nodes in a\r\ndifferent diagram than the simulation (a real structural difference E1 did not catch because E1 compares the\r\ncheckpoint graph with a sim built from the same resolver), i.e. the defect is in the artefact, and the 29 RBW bad\r\nwires include consequences of it.\r\n\r\nQuestions: (1) which explanation do log:660-711 and the plan rows (tools/bench/sim/l2a1/stageplan_l2a1.json:207-215 for\r\nsr1_L0 dst #5825 term 5883) support; (2) the cheapest separating check WITHOUT a new stage run (e.g. read the saved\r\nD1_l2_a1_20260925_235224.vi's graph and look up the diagram that owns #5825/#5702/#10750/#5725/#5967 vs Diagram[21]'s\r\nuid); (3) does PD184's rule (connect sinks = E1 + RBW) already cover these five rows so the P2 gate should skip them?\r\nFiles: tools/bench/stage_d1_l2a1_86-5.log, tools/recipes/stage_d1_l2a1.py, tools/stagekit.py (address 713-759,\r\ncfw_second_pass 848-864, expect_is_broken_false 686-707), tools/stagexec.py, tools/bench/sim/l2a1/stageplan_l2a1.json,\r\ndocs/d1-loop12-17-split-plan.md.\r\n\r\n\r\n--- HOW TO ANSWER (mandatory, from the dispatcher) ---\r\nYour job is to REFUTE the claim above, not to confirm it. Do not open with agreement.\r\n1. Name the single strongest reason the claim is WRONG.\r\n2. Name at least one ALTERNATIVE explanation of the same evidence.\r\n3. Name the observation that would FALSIFY the claim.\r\n4. End with the CHEAPEST discriminating test that separates the claim from your alternative.\r\nIf you still believe the claim holds after all four, say so explicitly and state what would change your mind.\r\n\r\n--- VERDICT CONTRACT (session protocol v1, C5; mandatory) ---\r\nWrite your answer as usual. Then make the LAST line of your answer exactly one line of the form\r\nVERDICT {\"schema\":\"verdict/1\",\"id\":\"hyp-l2a1-p2-86-5\",\"verdict\":\"<one of: refuted | supported | unverified>\",\"alternative\":\"...\",\"discriminating_test\":\"...\",\"violations\":[],\"sources\":[\"url or file:line\"],\"note\":\"\"}\r\nSingle-line JSON, no code fence. alternative/discriminating_test/note <= 300 chars each; violations <= 2 items {\"slug\",\"loss_min\",\"loss_usd\",\"evidence\"} using the retrospective slug list. Gates read ONLY this line; the prose above it is archived for people.\r\n\r\n\r\n## Answer\r\n\r\n**The claim is refuted.** The failures are an addressing defect in the recipe, as the claim says, but not the one it names. Diagram[21] is the correct diagram. The recipe clears the two fields `address()` needs to handle a tunnel, so the lookup could never succeed.\r\n\r\n**1. Strongest reason the claim is wrong.** All five sinks are `SelectorTunnel` **OuterTerminals**, not node terminals.\r\n- `graph_k_80_owners.json` lists them as `{\"uid\":5825,\"class\":\"SelectorTunnel\",\"owner\":\"CaseStructure\"}`, and the same for 5702, 10750, 5725 and 5967.\r\n- Their terminal rows (5883, 6038, 10760, 5741) all read `owner_class:\"SelectorTunnel\", term_class:\"OuterTerminal\"`.\r\n- A tunnel is owned by its Case structure, not by the diagram, so its uid is never in `Diagram[k].Nodes[]`, whichever diagram is named.\r\n- `Stage.address` already handles this. `tools/stagekit.py:728-739` maps a tunnel's outer terminal to its structure node, but only when `owner_class`/`term_class` say so.\r\n- The recipe hard-codes `\"owner_class\": \"\", \"term_class\": \"\"` (`tools/recipes/stage_d1_l2a1.py:90`), so that branch is skipped and the lookup fails at `stagekit.py:740-741`.\r\n\r\n**2. The diagram is correct.**\r\n- The tunnels share their x position with the Case structures that were moved: 5825, 5702 and 5725 sit at x=3771, the same x as CaseStructure #5540, which was moved to (3771,1033). #10750 sits at x=3305, the same x as #10445, which was moved to (3305,972).\r\n- Those moves landed on \"Diagram[21]\" (`stage_d1_l2a1_86-5.log:43,100`), which is dest #23166 (`stageplan_l2a1.json:19`).\r\n- So Diagram[21] holds the owning structures, and `frame_diagram` names the right diagram. The claim's first sub-hypothesis (\"wrong diagram / pre-move location\") is not supported, and neither is its second (uid→index picks a different diagram).\r\n- The competing explanation (\"the build left the sinks elsewhere\") is also not supported. The owners are where the plan put them, and E1 42/42 plus PB extra/missing [] agree.\r\n\r\n**Second latent defect.** `cfw_second_pass` calls `self.address(dst, False)` without `objs` (`stagekit.py:853`). Even with the class fields restored, the geometry lookup `pos[uid]` at line 730 would raise KeyError. There is a further question: `wired_terminals(dst[\"uid\"])` (line 855) and `node_terms(dd, dn)` would then address the structure node, so the terminal must be matched by `verify_term_uid` on the structure. Only the uid path at line 750 can do that reliably. The recipe's own comment \"tunnel/register **node** sinks\" (line 9) and its filter (line 84, which excludes only ControlTerminal) show the classification was assumed, not read.\r\n\r\n**Alternative explanation.** The P2 row filter picks the wrong set of rows. Rows whose sink is a tunnel's outer face should have been skipped under PD184, like the register-face rows at log 686-688, or routed through the structure path. They were sent down the plain node path instead.\r\n\r\n**3. What would falsify my reading (and support the claim).** A live read of `D1_l2_a1_20260925_235224.vi` showing that CaseStructure #5540 or #10445 is *not* in Diagram[21].Nodes[] (Diagram[21] uid ≠ 23166 at P2 time), or that a SelectorTunnel uid *does* appear in some diagram's Nodes[].\r\n\r\n**4. Your question (3), PD184 coverage.** PD184(a) lists \"register face\" among the E1 + RBW (+CT) only rows. These five sinks are a Case tunnel's outer face, the same kind of structure face. The recipe skipped the SR faces (log 686-688) but not these, so either the filter should skip them or it needs the structure-addressing path. That is a judgement call and I have not settled it. The artefact itself shows no fault: E1, PB and RBW all passed.\r\n\r\n**Cheapest discriminating test (no new stage run).** Run a headless `report_all('Diagram')` plus `node_labels` on the saved `D1_l2_a1_20260925_235224.vi` (md5 51d9b8a3). Check that Diagram[21] has uid 23166, that its Nodes[] contains #5540 and #10445, and that none of the five tunnel uids appears in any diagram's Nodes[]. Alternatively, dry-run `address()` on the same five ends with `owner_class=\"SelectorTunnel\", term_class=\"OuterTerminal\"` and `objs` passed. If it resolves to the structures with 1-hit terminals, the recipe's line 90 is the whole defect.\r\n\r\nThe web search did not settle whether Diagram.Nodes[] ever lists tunnels. The results cover only tunnel scripting in general ([NI forum: pass-through tunnel in a case structure](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/LabvVIEW-Scripting-wiring-a-pass-through-tunnel-inside-a-case/td-p/3945608), [LabVIEW Wiki: VI Scripting properties](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Category:VI_Scripting_Property)). The \"not in Nodes[]\" point rests on local evidence: the objects table lists these tunnels as owned by `CaseStructure`, and the `address()` docstring and code (`stagekit.py:715-719`) treat them the same way.\r\n\r\nSources:\r\n- [NI forum: pass-through tunnel scripting](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/LabvVIEW-Scripting-wiring-a-pass-through-tunnel-inside-a-case/td-p/3945608)\r\n- [LabVIEW Wiki: VI Scripting Property](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Category:VI_Scripting_Property)\r\n\r\nVERDICT {\"schema\":\"verdict/1\",\"id\":\"hyp-l2a1-p2-86-5\",\"verdict\":\"refuted\",\"alternative\":\"Diagram[21] (=#23166) is right: owners #5540/#10445 were moved there. The 5 sinks are SelectorTunnel OuterTerminals (not in Nodes[]); recipe:90 blanks owner_class/term_class so address():728-739 never maps tunnel->structure; objs also not passed (stagekit:853).\",\"discriminating_test\":\"Headless read of saved D1_l2_a1_20260925_235224.vi: Diagram[21] uid==23166, its Nodes[] holds #5540/#10445, no tunnel uid in any Nodes[]; or dry address() with owner_class=SelectorTunnel, term_class=OuterTerminal, objs passed.\",\"violations\":[],\"sources\":[\"tools/bench/sim/l2a1/graph_k_80_owners.json (objs 5825/5702/10750/5725/5967 SelectorTunnel owner CaseStructure; rows 5883/6038/10760/5741 OuterTerminal)\",\"tools/recipes/stage_d1_l2a1.py:90\",\"tools/stagekit.py:728-741\",\"tools/stagekit.py:853\",\"tools/bench/stage_d1_l2a1_86-5.log:43,100,689-711\",\"tools/bench/sim/l2a1/stageplan_l2a1.json:19\"],\"note\":\"Addressing defect confirmed but mis-located: not the diagram, the tunnel-vs-node class. Whether PD184 should skip tunnel outer faces is a judgement call; the artefact shows no build fault.\"}\r\n\r\n## Sources\r\n\r\n(extract from answer)\r\n\r\n## What was done with it\r\n\r\n2026-09-26, material card 86-5: nothing was changed in response. The card allowed one stage run and forbade editing stage_d1_l2a1.py and stagekit.py. The refutation is recorded as it stands: the diagram is right, and the five sinks are SelectorTunnel OuterTerminals that the P2 end cannot address, because stage_d1_l2a1.py:90 blanks owner_class and term_class and objs is not passed (stagekit.py:853). The judgement session must pick one of two fixes: have P2 skip tunnel outer faces under PD184, or pass the owner_class, term_class and objs so the second pass can address them. The saved artefact D1_l2_a1_20260925_235224.vi (md5 51d9b8a3) is kept, and no build fault is claimed against it.\r\n\r\ntools/bench/stage_d1_l2a1.json-733-  \"